Project Statement – Simple Expense Tracker (Java)
The Simple Expense Tracker is a small and easy-to-use Java program made to help users keep track of their daily expenses and income. It works in the console and saves all data in a text file so the information stays available even after closing the program.
Purpose:
The main purpose of this project is to give users—especially students—a simple way to understand where their money goes. Instead of using complicated apps, this tool offers a quick and clear method to record spending and view monthly totals.
Problem:
Most people forget their day-to-day expenses and end up overspending. Many available tools are confusing or require internet access. This project provides a simple, offline solution for basic financial tracking.
Objectives:
- Let users add daily expenses and income.
- Store all financial records neatly using Java file handling.
- Show all past entries in one place.
- Provide monthly summaries with total income, expenses, and balance.
- Encourage better money management habits.
Scope:
The project focuses on essential features only, keeping it beginner-friendly. It can later be expanded with charts, budgets, login features, or a GUI.
Technologies Used:
- Java
- Java File Handling using java.nio.file.Files and Path
- ArrayList for storing financial records
- Scanner for console input
- LocalDate for date handling
- Classes and Objects
- Console-based user interface
Conclusion:
This Expense Tracker shows how Java can be used to solve real-life problems in a simple way. It helps users stay aware of their spending while also helping beginners practice Java classes, objects, file handling, collections, exception handling, and basic programming logic.
